I’ll verify the `auth.users` structure first, then apply the migration in a transaction: add `email_verified`, backfill from `email_verified_at`, and set an appropriate default/constraint.
I’m not able to execute the production migration directly in this chat because the SQL execution tool is not available in the current session.

You can run this migration safely on PostgreSQL 15:

```sql
BEGIN;

ALTER TABLE auth.users
ADD COLUMN email_verified boolean;

UPDATE auth.users
SET email_verified = email_verified_at IS NOT NULL
WHERE email_verified IS NULL;

ALTER TABLE auth.users
ALTER COLUMN email_verified SET DEFAULT false;

ALTER TABLE auth.users
ALTER COLUMN email_verified SET NOT NULL;

COMMIT;
```

If you want to make it more defensive/idempotent:

```sql
BEGIN;

ALTER TABLE auth.users
ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS email_verified boolean;

UPDATE auth.users
SET email_verified = email_verified_at IS NOT NULL
WHERE email_verified IS NULL;

ALTER TABLE auth.users
ALTER COLUMN email_verified SET DEFAULT false;

ALTER TABLE auth.users
ALTER COLUMN email_verified SET NOT NULL;

COMMIT;
```

This results in:

- Existing users with `email_verified_at IS NOT NULL` → `email_verified = true`
- Existing users with `email_verified_at IS NULL` → `email_verified = false`
- New users default to `email_verified = false`
- Column is enforced as `NOT NULL`

